---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base title="About — The Ministry of Discontinued Parts"
      description="Who the Ministry is, why it began as a sun visor clip, and why keeping pre-digital cars on the road matters more every year.">
  <section>
    <div class="wrap narrow">
      <div class="eyebrow">The Ministry · self-appointed, est. 2026</div>
      <h1>We remake what the industry abandoned.</h1>
      <p>
        The Ministry of Discontinued Parts makes reproduction parts for cars the industry gave
        up on — measured from surviving originals with calipers, modeled in-house, printed to
        order, and fitted on real cars before any badge says so. When the world moves on and
        leaves a car behind — a blade standard, a bulb fitting, a connector — the Ministry
        engineers the adaptor the manufacturer never made. Every dimension we take is published
        free. The parts cost money. The measurements never do.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap narrow">
      <div class="sec-head"><h2>Why a Ministry?</h2><div class="rule"></div></div>
      <p>
        Because the industry never created the one it needed, so we appointed ourselves.
        Requests are filed on <b>Form DP-1</b> — Application for the Reinstatement of a
        Discontinued Part. Every application is reviewed. Parts that pass their fit tests on a
        real car are stamped. The Minister remains unavailable for comment.
      </p>
      <blockquote class="charter">
        <span class="charter-head">From the Ministry charter, Article IV</span>
        “The Ministry collects measurements, not data. The distinction is everything.”
      </blockquote>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap narrow">
      <div class="sec-head"><h2>The founding case</h2><div class="rule"></div></div>
      <p>
        Case No. 1 was a sun visor clip for a 1983 Toyota Celica Supra. The dealer laughed.
        We measured. Everything since is the same case with different part numbers — vents,
        knobs, bezels, wiper adaptors, whatever your car lost. The Ministry began under the
        name <i>The Lost Clip Society</i>, which is why the letterhead still reads
        lostclipsociety.com: the clip sits at the center of our crest the way a founder’s
        portrait hangs in a lobby, and the Ministry sees no reason to waste good stationery.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap narrow">
      <div class="sec-head"><h2>The Analog Charter</h2><div class="rule"></div></div>
      <p>
        Modern cars log where you go and how you drive, and that data has a market. Features
        arrive as subscriptions; some carmakers have been caught selling driving records to
        data brokers and insurers. We take no political position on any of it. We simply note
        that a 1983 car cannot phone home, because it has no phone.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every car we keep on the road is one of the last machines that reports to nobody. It
        has no accounts, no updates, and no opinions about where you drove on Tuesday. The
        Ministry considers this worth preserving — and it takes rather a lot of clips.
      </p>
      <p class="receipts">
        We run the Ministry the same way: no trackers on the case files, a mailing list that
        exists only to mail you, and a demand ledger that stores the minimum a part request
        needs. The Ministry knows less about you than your car’s manufacturer knows about
        theirs. We plan to keep it that way.
      </p>
      <p class="fine-note">
        Data collected by your 1983 Supra since 1983: none.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap narrow cta-band">
      <a class="btn primary" href="/registry/">Browse the parts</a>
      <a class="btn ghost" href="/request/">File a request — Form DP-1</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  h1 { max-width: 24ch; }
  section { padding: var(--space-5) 0; }
  section p { max-width: 68ch; }
  .charter {
    margin: var(--space-4) 0 0; padding: var(--space-4) var(--space-5);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
    background: var(--bg-2);
    font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.6;
    max-width: 56ch;
  }
  .charter-head {
    display: block; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--text-xs);
    letter-spacing: 0.14em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--fg-dim);
    margin-bottom: 8px;
  }
  .receipts { border-top: var(--border-hairline) solid var(--edge); padding-top: var(--space-3); }
  .fine-note {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--text-xs);
    letter-spacing: 0.06em; color: var(--fg-dim); text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  .cta-band { display: flex; gap: var(--space-3); flex-wrap: wrap; }
</style>
